LockingModel: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the resource locking model for the .sdd system — how exclusive
  and shared access to resources is managed, how locks are acquired and
  released, how deadlocks are prevented/detected, and how AI task
  collisions are prevented. This is reusable system intelligence;
  concrete lock state lives in projects/vkard.az/dependencies/locks.sdd.

Owns:
  Lock types, lock acquisition/release rules, deadlock detection,
  lease/timeout model, lock recovery, optimistic vs pessimistic locking,
  AI task collision prevention.

DoesNotOwn:
  Concrete lock instances (those live in projects/vkard.az/dependencies/locks.sdd),
  task execution (scheduler's job), resource provisioning (infrastructure's
  job).

LockTypes:

  EXCLUSIVE:
    Only one holder at a time. Blocks all other requests until released.
    Use for: mutations, writes, deployments, infrastructure changes.

  SHARED:
    Multiple holders allowed. Blocks only exclusive requests.
    Use for: reads, queries, analysis, documentation generation.

  OPTIMISTIC:
    No lock acquired upfront. Conflict detected at commit time.
    Use for: low-contention resources, human-edited files, documentation.

  LEASE:
    Time-limited exclusive lock. Automatically released after expiry
    unless renewed. Use for: long-running tasks, AI agent work.

  ADVISORY:
    Voluntary coordination. Does not block, but signals intent.
    Use for: planned maintenance, coordinated deployments.


LockStates:

  PENDING: lock requested, waiting for acquisition
  ACQUIRED: lock held by requester
  RELEASED: lock was held, now free
  EXPIRED: lease timed out, automatically released
  CONFLICT: lock acquisition blocked by existing lock
  DEADLOCK: lock cycle detected, resolution required


LockAcquisition:

  Request:
    lock:
      resource: <resource_id>
      type: EXCLUSIVE | SHARED | OPTIMISTIC | LEASE | ADVISORY
      requester: <task_id | agent_id>
      duration: <seconds> (for LEASE)
      renew: <boolean> (for LEASE)

  Evaluation:
    1. Check existing locks on resource
    2. Check compatibility matrix
    3. Check for deadlock potential
    4. If compatible → ACQUIRED
    5. If conflict → CONFLICT (queue or reject)
    6. If deadlock → DEADLOCK (resolve)


CompatibilityMatrix:

  Requester\Holder | EXCLUSIVE | SHARED | OPTIMISTIC | LEASE | ADVISORY
  EXCLUSIVE        |    NO     |   NO   |     YES    |  NO   |    YES
  SHARED           |    NO     |  YES   |     YES    |  NO   |    YES
  OPTIMISTIC       |   YES     |  YES   |     YES    |  YES  |    YES
  LEASE            |    NO     |   NO   |     YES    |  NO*  |    YES
  ADVISORY         |   YES     |  YES   |     YES    |  YES  |    YES

  * LEASE vs LEASE: compatible only if same requester or renew = false


PessimisticLocking:

  Lock acquired before access. Blocks until acquired or timeout.

  Use when:
    - high contention expected
    - conflict cost is high
    - resource is critical path

  Rules:
    [LK1] EXCLUSIVE lock MUST be acquired before mutation.
    [LK2] SHARED lock MUST be acquired before read if consistency
      required.
    [LK3] Lock timeout MUST be defined for all blocking acquisitions.
    [LK4] Failed acquisition MUST NOT block indefinitely.


OptimisticLocking:

  No lock acquired. Conflict detected at commit.

  Use when:
    - low contention expected
    - conflict cost is low
    - human-edited content

  Rules:
    [LK5] Optimistic access MUST include version/timestamp check at commit.
    [LK6] Conflict MUST trigger retry or escalation, not silent overwrite.
    [LK7] Optimistic lock is advisory — it signals intent but does not block.


LeaseModel:

  LEASE locks expire automatically.

  Lease lifecycle:
    ACQUIRE → (active) → RENEW | EXPIRE → RELEASE

  Rules:
    [LK8] LEASE lock MUST have explicit duration.
    [LK9] LEASE lock MAY be renewed if renew = true.
    [LK10] Expired LEASE MUST be automatically released.
    [LK11] Task using LEASE MUST handle expiration — cannot assume
      lock persists beyond duration.


DeadlockDetection:

  Cycle in lock wait graph = deadlock.

  Detection:
    On each lock request:
      1. Build wait-for graph
      2. Run cycle detection (DFS)
      3. If cycle found → DEADLOCK

  Prevention:
    [LK12] Lock ordering: resources MUST be acquired in canonical order.
    [LK13] Timeout: lock acquisition MUST timeout after max_wait.
    [LK14] Deadlock detection MUST run on every lock request.

  Resolution:
    [LK15] On deadlock: abort youngest transaction (shortest hold time).
    [LK16] Aborted task MUST release all held locks.
    [LK17] Deadlock event MUST be logged with full graph.


LockTimeout:

  All blocking acquisitions MUST have timeout.

  timeout:
    default: <configurable>
    max: <hard limit>
    on_timeout: WAIT | RETRY | ESCALATE | ABORT

  Rules:
    [LK18] Timeout MUST be shorter than dependent task's deadline.
    [LK19] Retry after timeout MUST use backoff (exponential recommended).
    [LK20] Repeated timeout SHOULD trigger escalation to human.


LockRecovery:

  When lock holder crashes or becomes unresponsive:

  1. Detect: heartbeat timeout or explicit failure
  2. Classify: LEASE (auto-expire) vs PERSISTENT (manual recovery)
  3. Recover:
     LEASE → wait for expiry or force-release after safety margin
     PERSISTENT → escalate to human or use recovery policy
  4. Notify: inform waiters of lock status change

  Rules:
    [LK21] LEASE recovery MUST NOT release before safety margin.
    [LK22] PERSISTENT lock recovery MUST require human approval unless
      policy defines automatic recovery.
    [LK23] Lock recovery MUST notify all waiting requesters.


AITaskCollisionPrevention:

  Multiple AI agents may attempt to work on same task/resource.

  Prevention:
    [LK24] AI agent MUST acquire lock before starting task work.
    [LK25] Lock request includes agent_id and task_id.
    [LK26] Second agent requesting same task/resource MUST wait or
      select alternative task.
    [LK27] Lock release MUST happen on task state transition to
      DONE, FAILED, or CANCELLED.

  Collision handling:
    [LK28] Detected collision → second agent receives TASK_LOCKED event.
    [LK29] Second agent selects next candidate from scheduler.
    [LK30] Human MAY override lock to reassign task.


Reservation:

  Reservation = planned lock acquisition.

  reservation:
    resource: <resource_id>
    requester: <task_id>
    type: EXCLUSIVE | SHARED
    start: <timestamp>
    end: <timestamp>
    status: REQUESTED | CONFIRMED | ACTIVE | COMPLETED | CANCELLED

  Rules:
    [LK31] Reservation does not block other reservations for different
      time windows.
    [LK32] Overlapping reservations for same resource MUST be validated
      for compatibility.
    [LK33] Reservation MUST convert to lock at start time.
    [LK34] Missed reservation (task not started) MUST be cancelled
      after grace period.


LockGovernance:

  [LK35] Lock acquisition MUST go through policy engine.
  [LK36] Human-requested locks have priority over AI-requested locks.
  [LK37] System locks (maintenance, backup) have highest priority.
  [LK38] Lock hold time SHOULD be minimized — acquire late, release early.
  [LK39] Lock audit log MUST record all acquisitions, releases, timeouts,
    conflicts, and deadlocks.


